Yes. The electricity is the same. What differs is the agreement about how to deliver it, and the machinery that turns it into something the device can use.

That agreement is where you’ll find real engineering, open standards, and proprietary lock-in. The move is to figure out which layer you’re looking at before you assume the whole thing has to be different.

What actually happens when you plug in a phone

We call the little brick a charger, but in a typical phone setup there are three separate jobs.

Part	Job
Wall adapter	Converts alternating current from the outlet into regulated direct-current power the device can use.
Cable	Carries power and, depending on the system, signals that help the device and adapter agree on what to deliver.
Charging circuit inside the phone	Takes that input, powers the device as needed, and controls how energy enters the battery.

An outlet supplies AC, meaning the voltage reverses direction periodically. The brick converts that into DC and regulates the output. The phone has its own circuitry to convert and distribute that power again. The screen, processor, and battery can all need different electrical conditions from the ones arriving at the port.

So the brick might supply 9 volts while the battery operates at a much lower voltage. There’s a conversion happening between them. That’s one of the most important ideas here. You can change how power is delivered without changing the energy source or the job you’re trying to do.

Volts, amps, watts, and watt-hours

Term	What it means in practice
Voltage	Energy available per unit of charge. It tells you something about the electrical conditions the input has to tolerate.
Current	How much charge moves per second.
Power	How fast energy is being transferred. For DC, watts equal volts times amps.
Energy	How much was transferred or stored in total. At a steady 10 watts for one hour, you transfer 10 watt-hours.

5 volts × 2 amps = 10 watts.
10 volts × 1 amp = 10 watts.

Same rate of energy transfer. Different electrical conditions. A circuit designed for 5 volts can be damaged by 10 volts even though the arithmetic comes out to the same wattage. On the other hand, when equipment is designed for it, raising the voltage lets you deliver the same power with less current, which reduces heating losses in the same wire. That’s a real engineering reason for offering several voltages.

Here’s another distinction that clears up a lot. On a compatible USB Power Delivery connection, “65 W” describes what the adapter can offer under supported conditions. A phone that can use 20 W can negotiate a supported level and draw what it needs. Think of the number on the brick as a ceiling for the right combination of voltage and current. The device, adapter, and cable all matter. 

A battery has its own rules

A lithium-ion battery is a chemical system. Charging drives lithium ions from one electrode toward the other while electrons move through the external circuit. The energy ends up stored in the battery’s chemical state. Getting that energy into the cell requires controlling the rate and respecting its voltage limit.

A common charging pattern has two main phases. The circuit first regulates the current while the battery voltage rises. Once it reaches the specified voltage, the circuit holds that voltage and the current tapers. That taper is one reason a device can gain charge quickly at first and take longer to finish. The power the brick can provide is only one part of the story. The battery’s present state and the device’s charging controls determine how much it can take. 

That gives us a critical difference between charging a device and charging a cell. A USB port on a device ordinarily leads to circuitry that manages the cell. A bare cell’s terminals are on the other side of that circuitry. The two points may both have wires and voltage. They’re asking for very different things. 

Where the vape example makes it click

Imagine three devices.

Device	Where the compatibility question lives
A phone with USB-C	The connector is standardized. Charging speed depends on the power modes the phone, adapter, and cable support.
A rechargeable vape with a USB-C input	It can share the same basic kind of USB power source if it supports that source’s output. Its internal charging circuit is designed for its own battery.
A small device with a magnetic charging dock	The dock might get power from ordinary USB, while the device-facing contacts, polarity, alignment, and division of charging functions are specific to that design.

The interesting case is that magnetic dock. Imagine it uses a familiar USB brick on one side and a proprietary snap-on connection on the other. The same product can be open at one boundary and closed at the next. A manufacturer can reuse commodity power upstream and control the accessory downstream.

You can also get the reverse kind of confusion. Two ports look identical because they’re both USB-C, but they support different features. USB-C describes the connector. USB Power Delivery describes a method for agreeing on power. Two cables can fit the same socket and have different power or data capabilities. A familiar shape answers “Will it plug in?” The specification answers “What can these pieces actually do together?” 

How much of this is companies making things proprietary?

Sometimes you’re absolutely seeing a business choice. If a company controls the replaceable cable, dock, cartridge, or accessory, it can capture sales after the original purchase. But a specialized interface might also solve a packaging problem. A tiny sealed device has different space constraints than a laptop. A magnetic mount may align itself in a way that makes sense for the product. A design can solve a real problem and give the company control over accessory sales.

The question I’d use is “What does this difference buy, and who gets the benefit?”

* A smaller connector might buy room for a bigger battery.
* A different charging profile might buy faster charging within the product’s thermal limits.
* A common connector might buy customers fewer cables and the ability to use what they already own.
* A unique connector might buy the company accessory revenue while making the customer depend on it for replacements.

You can get a clearer answer by asking what would happen if the company used a standard interface. Would the device get bigger? Would it lose a feature? Would customers simply have more places to buy a replacement? Those are different consequences, and now you can argue about something concrete.

How to leverage it

Compare interfaces before comparing products. When you hear “only our part works,” figure out which boundary actually imposes the restriction.

1. Identify the job. Are you delivering power, transferring data, securing a part, or charging a cell?
2. Find where the control lives. Does the wall brick regulate the input? Does the device manage the battery? Does a dock translate between the two?
3. Read the contract at the boundary. For a charging setup, that means checking the documented input and output modes, connector, protocol, and cable capability.
4. Find the shared part. A product-specific dock might still work with an ordinary compatible USB adapter. A faster brick may be interchangeable at basic speed even if the device’s fastest mode requires a particular combination.
5. Check what the substitute actually preserves. “It charges” and “it charges at full speed while the device is running” are two different outcomes.

That’s the practical buying move. If your phone charges slowly, another high-wattage brick might help. Or the existing brick may already have enough power and the limit is the cable, the lack of a shared fast-charging mode, or the phone tapering as the battery fills. Find the bottleneck before buying more capacity.

The product-building move is bigger. If you’re designing a product, you get to choose where to make it easy to connect and where to do something distinctive. A startup can inherit a whole ecosystem by using the standard where customers already have equipment, then spend its engineering budget on the experience people actually came for. Conversely, if the interface itself is the innovation, make the improvement concrete enough that customers can feel what they’re getting for the cost of switching.

The same pattern across domains

Domain	Shared mechanism or infrastructure	Interface that determines what works	Where you can get leverage
Software	Programs store, process, and exchange data.	File formats, schemas, APIs, and permissions.	Keep the underlying records in a portable format. Translate at the boundary when you switch tools.
Wild Hawg	People already understand buying a portable caffeine product.	“Energy drink in a bag” tells a new customer what role it fills.	Borrow a familiar buying decision. Put the novelty into the taste, texture, portability, and brand experience they actually get.
Business operations	People and systems can do work.	Who owns the job, what authority they have, and what counts as complete.	Give the person what the task requires. Adding talent may leave an approval bottleneck exactly where it was.
Language	People can exchange information and make commitments.	The frame, meaning of key terms, and what a reply is taken to accept.	Expose the assumption before you answer. “At this instant or over the whole trip?” fixes the frame behind 0 versus 23 mph.
Pantheon	A reader can interpret a text and use it to produce something.	What a module takes in, which terms depend on context, and what result it should produce.	Keep the idea portable. Spell out which piece needs translating when you move it from one model, audience, or task to another.

An adapter in any of these domains has a scope. A software adapter might translate field names perfectly while losing which records were authorized for sharing. A product slogan can carry the role of an energy drink over to a gummy without carrying over the experience of drinking something cold. “Show me what changed” travels from code review to a family conversation, but what counts as evidence changes with it.

So whenever you move a pattern between domains, ask “What has to survive the translation for this to still do its job?” A good adapter preserves what matters and gives you a way to see what it lost.

This is your “unequipped” idea in engineering form

Connect a capable phone to a low-power source while it’s running a demanding app. The source may supply some power while the battery still drains because the phone is using energy faster than the source supplies it. Everything can be functioning, and the setup can still come up short for the job.

Now take “Can you handle the launch?” and unpack it like an electrical specification.

* Who can approve the final version?
* How much time is available?
* Who can fix a problem on launch day?
* What other work is drawing on the same people?
* What counts as a successful launch?

The person might have the skill to run it. The setup might give them responsibility without decision rights or a team without time. Unequipped gives you a place to look and a variable you can change. If supplying the missing authority, information, or time changes the outcome, you’ve learned more than you would from asking them to try harder.

The strongest move is to find the boundary where an input becomes a result. That’s where you can substitute a commodity part, build an adapter, improve the design, or discover the one resource that was missing. Sometimes you need a better device. Sometimes all you needed was a way to deliver the power you already had.